# retrylint

`retrylint` checks the retry policy of an HTTP client before it ships. A policy file lists one endpoint per line: the
request method, the path, and how many times the client retries a failed request.

```text
# method path retries
GET /orders 3
POST /orders 3
```

Run it on the file:

```bash
python3 -m retrylint client.conf
```

It prints one warning per endpoint whose retries can repeat a side effect, and exits with status 1 when it printed any.
A retry repeats the request, so it is only harmless for a method that RFC 9110 defines as idempotent (section 9.2.2):
GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, TRACE, PUT, and DELETE. POST and PATCH are not idempotent.

Run the tests with `python3 -m unittest`.

